  "layout_note": "Single wide text-column: the closing ARGUMENT of the Book of Iob (running commentary continued from the previous page). NOT 2-column scripture and NOT verse-structured — no verses, verse-numbers, or dagger markers. The column MIXES two faces that alternate within lines — doctrinal/emphatic phrases in ROMAN (upright), connective narration in ITALIC (slanted). Left margin carries 6 roman-type side-notes (marginalia). Page closes with three decorative (broken) horizontal double-rules, then a centered roman colophon 'THE END OF THE FIRST TOME.', with a small centred 'L'/hook-shaped signature/ornament mark below it (uncertain, line 37). No errata/faults-escaped list and no printer's device/imprint on this page. TWO glyph regimes: ITALIC spans set 'w' as 'vv' and use long-ſ (per GUIDELINES §footnote-typeface); ROMAN spans use a real 'w' and upright letterforms. Lowercase roman real-'w' instances: 'with impietie' (l.0) and 'with fulnes of' (l.23). Capital W is set 'VV' in BOTH faces (italic 'VVhom' l.13, 'VVho' l.20, 'VVorkes' l.4, 'VVordes' l.33, 'VVil' l.34). column:'L'/'R' intentionally NOT applied — the page is objectively single-column, so imposing false 2-column structure would corrupt the gold data (deviation from the task's '2-column' hint flagged per diplomatic-fidelity / no-silent-degradation). Digitization marks excluded: 'www.fatimamovement.com' watermark and red papal-keys shield at bottom-left; printed folio '1114' at top-left.",
  "body": [
    {
      "line_index": 0,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "with impietie, impatience, ignorance, pride, blaſphemie, and obſti-",
      "line_end_hyphen": true,
      "has_uncertain": true,
      "note": "continues a paragraph carried over from the previous page. 'with impietie ... blaſphemie' is set in ROMAN (upright) → real 'w'; the line switches to italic at 'and obſti-'. (Corrects a prior pass that read the opening 'with' as italic 'vvith'.)"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 1,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "nacie, vices farre from Iobs ſanctitie, dilating alſo of Gods iuſtice, mercie,"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 2,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "vviſdome, povvre, and prouidence; and that no man ought to contend, nor ex-",
      "line_end_hyphen": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 3,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "poſtulate vvith God, that afflictions muſt be borne patiently, and that God is"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 4,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "iuſt, and maruelous in his VVorkes, vvherof no vviſeman euer doubted ; and ſo",
      "has_uncertain": true,
      "note": "'VVorkes' (God's Works) is a cap-height italic W → 'VV'; contrast lowercase 'workes of nature' at line 8 ('vvorkes')"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 5,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "Iob conuinced him vvith ſilence."
    },
    {
      "line_index": 6,
      "role": "prose",
      "paragraph_start": true,
      "text": "But God himſelf for deciſion of al (from ch. 38. to the end of the Booke)"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 7,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "firſt by vvay of examining inſtructed Iob more particularly, reciting manie"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 8,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "maruelous vvorkes of nature, ſhevving therby his Diuine Maieſtie, Povvre, and"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 9,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "vviſdome, exerciſing Iob in more patience, and vvithal perfecting"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 10,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "him in humilitie, ſo that vvith al reuerent feare and ſubiection, he offered"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 11,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "and ſubmitted him ſelfe to Gods onlie good pleaſure. Then finally God gaue"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 12,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "ſentence that Iob had defended the truth, & his three freindes had"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 13,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "erred. VVhom after Sacrifice, and Iobs prayer for them, he pardoned; reſtored"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 14,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "Iob to health, and to duble proſperitie, of al he had loſt before, geuing him alſo"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 15,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "long life, and a happie end."
    },
    {
      "line_index": 16,
      "role": "prose",
      "paragraph_start": true,
      "text": "In this hiſtorie beſides the literal ſenſe, ſhevving that Iob vvas iuſt and ſin-",
      "line_end_hyphen": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 17,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "cere, and not for his ſinnes (as his freindes falſly ſuppoſed) but for his more me-",
      "line_end_hyphen": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 18,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "rite vvas moſt extremly afflicted, and aftervvards reſtored to health and"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 19,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "vvealth : vve haue alſo here in the Allegorical ſenſe, an eſpecial figure of"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 20,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "Chriſt. VVho as he vvas abſolutly moſt innocent, & moſt perfect : ſo vvas he vvith-",
      "line_end_hyphen": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 21,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "out cōpariſon moſt afflicted of al mankind. Likevviſe Iobs reſtauration to better"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 22,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "ſtate then before, ſignified in the. Anagogical ſenſe, the Reſurrection, and",
      "has_uncertain": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 23,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "reſtauratiō of better, & moſt glorious qualities in the bleſſed, with fulnes of",
      "note": "'with fulnes of' is set in ROMAN (upright) type → real 'w', unlike the italic body's 'vv'"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 24,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "daies, in eternal glorie. Finally in the Moral ſenſe (vvhich S. Gregorie moſt"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 25,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "eſpecially proſecuteth) al Chriſtians haue here a moſt notable example of al"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 26,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "vertues, namely of patience, vvherin Iob proceded by degrees to great perfe-",
      "line_end_hyphen": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 27,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "ction. For he vvas firſt tried by the loſſe of al his goodes & children, and vvas"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 28,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "proued to be very patient. He vvas then moſt greuouſly tormented in bodie, &"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 29,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "being left vvithout comforth, albeit he lamentably bevvailed ſo great extre-",
      "line_end_hyphen": true,
      "has_uncertain": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 30,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "mitie, vviſhing ſuch daies had bene preuented; yet he neither ſpoke againſt God,",
      "has_uncertain": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 31,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "nor good man, nor his ovvne ſoule, & according to truth auouched & defended"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 32,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "his ovvne innocencie. And at laſt by Gods inſpiration, and ſvvete conſolation,"
    },
    {
      "line_index": 33,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "he reprehended him ſelf, of former imperfections vttered in ſome VVordes,",
      "has_uncertain": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 34,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "and vvith ful reſignation to Gods VVil, ſuſteyned al his loſſes and paynes, not",
      "has_uncertain": true
    },
    {
      "line_index": 35,
      "role": "prose",
      "text": "only vvith contentment, but alſo vvith ioy."
    },
    {
      "line_index": 36,
      "role": "colophon",
      "text": "THE END OF THE FIRST TOME."
    },
    {
      "line_index": 37,
      "role": "signature",
      "text": "⟨?⟩",
      "has_uncertain": true,
      "note": "small centred 'L'/hook-shaped mark on the centre-line below the colophon; deliberate-looking but unidentifiable — a signature/register mark, a small ornament, or stray ink. Not a right-justified catchword (this is the final leaf of the tome). Flagged, not guessed."
    }
  ],
